WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.390
Sanger spent twelve years on a single molecule.

2
00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:11.810
He worked on Tennis Court Road in Cambridge, a Quaker conscientious objector who had

3
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:16.130
spent the war scrubbing hospital toilets with a mop, and he called himself

4
00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:20.550
"academically not brilliant" he had taken three years over the first part of his

5
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:21.550
degree.

6
00:00:21.683 --> 00:00:26.703
Insulin fought him: its disulfide bonds rearranged themselves mid-experiment, the

7
00:00:26.703 --> 00:00:31.203
molecule effectively lying to him about its own structure, until he caught them with

8
00:00:31.203 --> 00:00:33.363
a trace of thioglycolic acid.

9
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:40.727
In 1955 he published the answer 51 amino acids, two chains, three sulfur bridges, in

10
00:00:40.727 --> 00:00:41.787
one fixed order.

11
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:49.080
He did it again with DNA in 1977, sequenced the first complete DNA genome, and took

12
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.780
a second Nobel in 1980, the only person of the century to win the chemistry prize

13
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.600
twice. He declined a knighthood because he did not want to be different, and retired

14
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.100
at 65 to his garden.

15
00:01:00.883 --> 00:01:05.163
When the great genome center at Hinxton asked to take his name, his only condition

16
00:01:05.163 --> 00:01:09.323
was a sentence that could stand as his whole creed: it had better be good.
